# gate-suites — the suites gate-spira.sh runs, and why each one earns the wait.
#
# NOTE: gate-spira.sh is NOT the production landing gate (sp-wyep). The landing gate is the
# fence CMD in the repo-map. This file names the suites that gate-spira.sh runs on a schedule;
# a red here files a bead but does not block landings.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY HAND-WRITTEN LIST OF SUITES, and it is deliberately not a list of what
# EXISTS. What exists is a glob over `spira/test-*.sh`; this file names the subset that
# gate-spira.sh runs on the timed pass, which is a choice somebody has to make and cannot be
# derived. Everything the glob finds and this file does not name is run by `suites.sh` on a
# timer without gate-spira.sh — so the complement is derived, and a suite dropped from here
# moves to the timed run rather than falling out of the world.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS SHAPE EXISTS TO PREVENT. The list used to live inside gate-spira.sh, and
# nothing else could read it, so the timed runner could not know what the gate already
# covered and the only way to answer "what runs nowhere" was for a person to compare two
# things by eye. Nobody did, for as long as the arrangement lasted: five of nine suites ran
# nowhere at all, three of them landed the same night citing themselves as verification.
#
# A LINE HERE MUST NAME A FILE THAT EXISTS. gate-spira.sh refuses to report a pass when one
# does not, because a gate that quietly runs five checks where six are written down is a gate
# reporting on work it never looked at.
#
# A SUITE NAMED HERE IS FIXED IN THE COMMIT THAT BREAKS IT, OR DELETED WITH THE GAP RECORDED
# HERE — never left running and red with a note explaining why. Red-with-an-explanation is not
# a third option: it masks every future regression in the code it covers while costing a slice
# on every branch, and a stale note reads identically to a live one.
#
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored. One repository-relative path per line.

# THE VERDICT CACHE'S OWN, and it earns its place by being the one check between a branch and
# a landing that decides whether any of the others run. A cache that answers a question it was
# never asked lands work on a trial nobody performed, and it does it silently: a reused verdict
# and a real pass are the same exit status, in the same words, at the same cost as success. Five
# seconds, and it plants an offender for every side of the key.
spira/test-gate-verdict.sh

# THE HOST-ESCAPE FENCE'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. host-check.sh requires every suite to run
# in a container or carry a # host-reason: declaration; this suite plants an undeclared host
# suite (SEEN RED), withdraws it (SEEN GREEN), and verifies the count suites.sh renders.
# host-check.sh replaces both hermetic.sh's main scan and the fixture-contamination fence
# (both deleted in sp-1ctx): a suite without a declaration cannot be added silently, which
# is the class of defect the contamination fence existed to detect after the fact.
spira/test-host-reason.sh

# THE ATTEMPT LEDGER'S OWN. An attempt count decides whether work is poisoned, so a count
# that charges the harness for its own restarts removes work nobody ever tried.
spira/test-attempts.sh

# THIS GATE'S OWN YIELD METER, and it is the condition on the gate being here at all. A check
# may sit between work and its landings only while it is catching real defects
# (law-gate-earns-its-place); yield.sh is the count that makes that sentence actionable, and
# the specific way it fails is by silently ceasing to be called — which renders as a tidy "no
# gate faults" and reads as good news. The suite plants a real defect and a red that is a
# suite reading the box, and requires them in different columns.
spira/test-yield.sh

# THE SOP LINT FENCE'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. gate-spira.sh calls sop.sh lint as a fence to
# catch SOPs written directly with `bd remember` — the back door that bypasses the write
# validator. Without a positive control, a lint that passes an empty shelf and a lint that
# cannot detect anything look identical. This suite plants a malformed SOP via the back door
# and requires lint to name it, then confirms the gate's call exists in gate-spira.sh.
spira/test-sop-lint.sh

# THE GATE'S OWN BUDGET ENFORCEMENT. gate-spira.sh times itself and files a bead when it
# overruns SPIRA_GATE_BUDGET; this suite proves the timing, the bead filing, and the dedup.
# In the timed suite run it consistently exceeds the per-suite slice (it invokes gate-spira.sh
# internally, whose own suites add up beyond the slice) and is killed as a false red.
# Moving it here ends that loop: gated suites run without a per-suite slice and this suite
# can reach its natural completion.

# THE LITERAL-LINT FENCE'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. A static check that reports a clean tree
# is indistinguishable from one whose matcher never fires, so this suite plants an offender
# in a scratch repository, requires the fence to name it, withdraws it, and only then reads
# the shipped tree's silence as evidence of a clean tree. Without this, the fence could fail
# to match for any reason and the gate would call it a pass.
spira/test-literal-lint.sh

# THE SCRATCH-FENCE'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. scratch-fence.sh refuses sp-* notes and
# *.fixed artefacts at the repository root — aeon working notes that ship to every consumer
# and cost suite time on every branch that follows them. This suite plants each pattern
# (SEEN RED), withdraws it (SEEN GREEN), and confirms the fence is referenced in gate-spira.sh.
# Without this, a fence whose matcher never fires is indistinguishable from one that passes
# a clean tree.
spira/test-scratch-fence.sh

# SCRATCH-FENCE AT CERTIFICATION: a root-level scratch file is refused before a CERTIFIED
# record is written. The cost of a fence violation escaping certification is a full CI run
# (image build + suites) blocked for every branch in the batch — sp-zmd3u-sop-verification.md
# demonstrated this in PR 59. The fence runs as part of the certification gate; this suite
# verifies that a branch with a root-level sp-* file is refused (gate called, bead reopened,
# file named in the note) and that a clean branch is still certified (positive control).
spira/test-cert-lint.sh

# THE SUITE-STATE FENCE'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. suite-state-fence.sh refuses a landing
# that carries a malformed lifecycle entry or a quarantine against a CLOSED bead (which
# has no exit path — hygiene requires LANDED, which CLOSED never satisfies). This suite
# plants each class of offender (SEEN RED), withdraws it (SEEN GREEN), and confirms that
# gate-spira.sh is wired to call the fence (law-a-documented-control-must-exist).
spira/test-suite-state-fence.sh

# THE MISSING-CMD PREFLIGHT'S OWN POSITIVE CONTROL. gate.sh resolves every bash <path>
# in the repo-map gate command against the base tree before running any trial; a path absent
# from the base returns a distinct config-error verdict rather than BASE_FAIL. This class of
# defect (gate command naming a file not yet on the base) blocked 22 branches with a
# misleading verdict (sp-lkzl). This suite plants an absent path (SEEN RED), requires the
# named file to appear in the output, and confirms the verdict is not BASE_FAIL. The positive
# control adds the file to the base and requires the gate to reach the suites.
spira/test-gate-missing-cmd.sh

# THE LANDING REBASE LIFECYCLE'S OWN FIXTURE INITIALIZATION. test-landing-rebase.sh (240s)
# covers rebase_branch classification, the survivor sweep, the loop-defer guard, and checkout
# refresh — all critical harness mechanisms. Uses server-mode testdb (+84s initialization
# overhead), so shares a 420s timed budget with 45 other tests; when queued late, it receives
# insufficient time and is killed by the watchdog. Running in the gate with a dedicated
# per-suite timeout (600s) gives it the wall time its server-mode fixture requires.
spira/test-landing-rebase.sh

